<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Manage Time – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning to Manage Time</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify the main ideas of a complex dialogue in media, provided there is an opportunity for clarification and repetition. Interactive: Identify the main ideas of feedback provided to solve problems in group settings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow a complex dialogue in a podcast about time management strategies, identify key points, and evaluate their usefulness (e.g., "The speaker recommended avoiding distractions by studying in a quiet space. How effective is this for your environment?"). Can listen to a peer's summary of study habits and identify main ideas (e.g., "He said the best way is to break down big tasks into smaller, more manageable ones."). Can determine the key advice from feedback on study routines (e.g., "It's important to have a set routine, like studying at the same time each day.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images related to studying, calendars, and students feeling stressed or successful. Students are asked: "Look at these pictures. What do you think this lesson will be about? What are your biggest *challenges* when it comes to managing your *study time*? Have you *ever procrastinated* on an *assignment*?" (5 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the core vocabulary for the lesson: *study, habits, focus, time, manage, procrastinate, strategies, improve, achieve, goals*. The teacher models the pronunciation of these words, focusing on contrastive sounds (e.g., /sk/ in "skill," /st/ in "study," /s/ in "success," /t/ in "time") and the pronunciation of '-ed' endings in example sentences (e.g., "I *have developed* better *habits*," "I *used to procrastinate* a lot"). The teacher explicitly explains the meaning of each word with simple gestures and short example sentences. (5 minutes)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the key vocabulary using Concept Checking Questions.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you *procrastinate*, do you do things immediately or later?" (Later)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a *strategy*? Is it a random idea or a plan?" (A plan)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When you *achieve* a *goal*, have you finished it or are you still working on it?" (Finished it)</li>
                </ul>
                (3 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An authentic podcast excerpt (approx. 2-3 minutes) titled "Crushing Your Classes: Top Time Management Strategies" featuring two high school students discussing their study routines, challenges with procrastination, and successful strategies they have adopted. The register is informal but academic-relevant. The format is a dialogue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Identify the main ideas of a complex dialogue in media, provided there is an opportunity for clarification and repetition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen once and answer: "What is the main topic of this podcast episode?" and "What are the two main problems the students discuss related to *study habits*?" (e.g., "The main topic is time management strategies." "They discuss *procrastination* and staying *focused*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True (T) or False (F) based on the dialogue. (e.g., "One student *used to procrastinate* on *assignments* until the last minute." (T) "They suggest studying for three hours straight without breaks." (F) "The Pomodoro Technique is mentioned as an *effective strategy*." (T))</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Information Matching):</b> Students listen a third time to match problems with *strategies* discussed. (e.g., Match 'Difficulty focusing' with 'Study in a quiet space'; Match 'Feeling overwhelmed' with 'Break down *goals* into smaller *tasks*'; Match 'Missing deadlines' with 'Create a consistent *routine*').</li>
                </ul>
                (12 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific details, using Present Perfect for achievements, Past Simple for reflecting on habits, WH-questions, and target vocabulary (*study, habits, focus, time, goals, grades, progress, learning, achievements, skills, routines, assignments, strategies, productivity, improvement, management, results, effort, success, inspiration*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill Listening):</b> The teacher provides a transcript of short excerpts from the podcast with blanks. Students listen carefully and fill in the missing words, focusing on target vocabulary and grammatical structures (e.g., "I *have developed* a new *strategy*." "I *used to procrastinate* on *assignments*." "What *strategies* *have you tried*?"). (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled (Sentence Reconstruction):</b> The teacher plays sentences from the podcast. After each sentence, students work in pairs to rephrase or summarize the main idea using at least one new target vocabulary word or a different grammatical structure (Present Perfect/Past Simple). For example, if the audio says, "I really struggled to start my homework on time," students might say, "She *used to procrastinate* with her *assignments*." Or, if the audio says, "I've started using a planner," students might say, "She *has developed* an *organized* *routine*." (5 minutes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Advice Listening):</b> Students are paired up. Student A describes a specific *study habit* or challenge they *used to* have or *have improved*. Student B listens and identifies the main idea of the challenge/improvement, then *suggests* a possible *strategy* from the podcast or their own experience. They switch roles. Focus on listening for the main point of the peer's statement and offering relevant advice. (e.g., Student A: "I *used to* always lose *focus* when *studying* at home." Student B: "So you had trouble staying *focused*. The podcast *suggested* finding a quiet place. Have you *tried* that?") (5 minutes)</li>
                </ul>
                (15 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group discussion and collaborative problem-solving based on listening to a hypothetical student's feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher divides students into small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays a short (1-minute) audio recording of a fictional student, "Carlos," explaining his *study habits* and struggles, particularly with *procrastination* and setting *goals*. (e.g., "I *used to* start big projects the night before. My *grades* *have improved* a little, but I still struggle to *manage my time* for all my *assignments*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In their groups, students collaboratively listen and discuss the main problems Carlos expresses. They should identify 2-3 key issues. (e.g., "Carlos said he *used to procrastinate*." "He needs to *improve* his *time management* for *assignments*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Then, each group *suggests* 2-3 specific *strategies* or pieces of advice for Carlos, drawing upon the vocabulary and concepts from the lesson and the podcast. They must use Present Perfect and Past Simple in their discussion. (e.g., "He *should* break down his *assignments* into smaller *tasks*. We *have discussed* this *strategy*." "He *might* *organize* his *time* better with a schedule. I *used to* have similar problems.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group briefly presents their top *suggestions* to the class, while other groups listen and *evaluate* the advice.</li>
                </ol>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students are actively applying time management strategies to solve a realistic problem, embodying "Study Habits for Success" and "Learning to Manage Time."</p>
                (10 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion and a quick exit ticket.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an exit ticket. They listen to a final short statement (30 seconds) from the teacher or a pre-recorded clip summarizing one key *time management strategy*. Students then write down:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The main idea of the *strategy* mentioned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One way they *could* apply it to their *study habits*.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Identifies the main idea of the strategy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 2 target vocabulary words (e.g., *strategy, manage, focus, improve*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Expresses their application clearly.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new *strategy* you *have learned* today that you *might* try to *improve* your *study habits*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do you feel you *listened* and understood the main *ideas* in our discussions today (1-5, 5 being excellent)?"</li>
                </ul>
                (5 minutes)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson helped you most to *focus* on listening for main *ideas* and specific *strategies*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *could* you use your *improved* listening *skills* outside of the classroom to *manage* your *time* and *study* more *effectively*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What *activities* worked best to engage students in listening comprehension and identifying main ideas? Did students effectively use the target vocabulary and grammar (Present Perfect/Past Simple) when discussing *study habits*? What adjustments are needed for supporting listening accuracy, especially regarding identifying key advice from feedback? What scaffolds *might* be needed for less proficient listeners?
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension) will use a written text about *time management strategies* and *study habits*. Students will read an article featuring some of the same core vocabulary and *strategies* introduced today, deepening their understanding through a different skill focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>